CLR (rocclr) rocvirtual.cpp (gfx803): make the pinned-memory-release
marker a system-scope barrier so the pinned buffer is unlocked only after
the copy shader's writes have drained from L2.

WHY
---
A torch `.cpu()`/`.to('cpu')` D2H copy on gfx803 faults with "Memory
access fault ... Page not present" (write from TC) under alloc/free churn,
on both the 7.14 and 10.0 lines (same torch 2.13; the 6.4.4 line does not
fault and is data-correct). Bisected to the CLR's pinned D2H path:
KernelBlitManager::readBuffer pins the pageable host destination
(hsa_amd_memory_lock) and a blit/copy shader writes that pinned buffer
directly. The pinned buffer is then released via
SchedulePinnedMemoryRelease, whose marker dispatched a NOP barrier
(kCacheStateIgnore): the marker's CL_COMPLETE fires when the copy kernel
retires, but the shader's TC stores to the pinned host buffer drain from
L2 to host memory only at a system-scope boundary. The subsequent
hsa_amd_memory_unlock (mapping removal) therefore races the write-back,
and the GPU's late drain faults on the now-unmapped page.

Controlled evidence: disabling host pinning (GPU_PINNED_MIN_XFER_SIZE
huge, or GPU_PINNED_XFER_SIZE=0) forces the clean staging path (copy
shader writes a GPU staging buffer; the fmm keeps it mapped) and the fault
disappears 3/3; the pinned path faults 5/5 on the same loop. The sync
(else) branch of addPinnedMem already does releaseGpuMemoryFence() before
release -- a system-scope barrier plus CPU wait -- which is exactly what
the async marker path was missing.

WHAT
---
SchedulePinnedMemoryRelease's marker gets kCacheStateSystem instead of
kCacheStateIgnore. submitMarker's kCacheStateSystem branch dispatches
kBarrierPacketHeader (HSA_FENCE_SCOPE_SYSTEM acquire+release), whose
completion signal fires only after the L2 has been flushed to system
scope -- so by the time CL_COMPLETE runs ReleasePinnedMemoryCallback and
unlocks the pinned buffer, no shader write to it can still be in flight.
This is the same fence the codebase already relies on elsewhere
(dispatchBarrierPacket(kBarrierPacketHeader), releaseGpuMemoryFence).

gfx803-family-specific only in that gfx803 is where the race was
confirmed; the unflushed-marker-vs-pinned-unlock ordering is generic CLR
code, so the fix is strictly safer on every line.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (gfx803 box, 2026-08-30): the D2H churn fault
is fixed by the combination of this patch with va-reuse-defer-mapping and
d2h-staged-copy; on its own it only makes the batch-pinned-release marker
ordering safe (a system-scope barrier before the pinned buffer is
unlocked).
diff --git a/projects/clr/rocclr/device/rocm/rocvirtual.cpp b/projects/clr/rocclr/device/rocm/rocvirtual.cpp
--- a/projects/clr/rocclr/device/rocm/rocvirtual.cpp
+++ b/projects/clr/rocclr/device/rocm/rocvirtual.cpp
@@ -3621,5 +3631,15 @@ void VirtualGPU::SchedulePinnedMemoryRelease(amd::HostQueue& queue,
     return;
   }
 
-  marker->setCommandEntryScope(amd::Device::kCacheStateIgnore);
+  // The marker must carry a SYSTEM-scope release fence, not the default
+  // kCacheStateIgnore NOP barrier: the pinned buffer this callback releases
+  // was just written by a blit/copy shader whose TC stores to it drain from
+  // L2 to host memory only at a system-scope boundary. A NOP-barrier
+  // completion fires before that drain, so the subsequent
+  // hsa_amd_memory_unlock (mapping removal) races the write-back and the
+  // GPU faults with "page not present" on gfx803 (confirmed via a
+  // repeatable torch .cpu()/D2H churn fault). The kCacheStateSystem branch
+  // in submitMarker dispatches a system-scope barrier whose completion
+  // implies the L2 flush finished, so the release below is safe.
+  marker->setCommandEntryScope(amd::Device::kCacheStateSystem);
   constexpr bool kNonBlockingCallback = false;
